# function readX(point: Point) { return point.x }  function guessX(point) { return point.x }, with Point inferred from its uses  const points: Point[] = [{ x: 1.5 }]  function firstX() { return points[0].x }
# Nobody lies here. A validating build ends the process where a value has another type than the one that is proven of it. Where a type is only expected, a test stands in front of the typed code,
# and a value of another type is an ordinary event: the test fails, the generic code runs, and the program prints what plain JavaScript prints. A validating build takes that way out like any build.
# The three places where a test stands: the entry of a typed function (`readX`), the use of a parameter whose type nothing checks (`guessX`), and a conversion in the middle of a body (`firstX`).
# Each gets a value of its type first, then an object of another layout, and the first two a primitive
# FORM: AsRead
